<template>
  <div class="rag-story">
    <h1 data-scroll-reveal>A Structure-Preserving RAG Ingestion Pipeline</h1>

    <article data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="110">
      <p class="lede">
        At PwC, I helped design a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) system for public enquiries built on
        complex source documents. My focus was the knowledge-base ingestion pipeline, the part that turns those
        documents into searchable chunks before retrieval. For the answers to be reliable, each chunk had to keep
        the conditions, qualifications, and decision paths that gave a rule its meaning.
      </p>
      <p>
        The PDFs made that difficult. Standard parsing could split merged or cross-page tables, break a paragraph
        at a page boundary, detach a Note or Remark from the sentence it qualified, or leave a flowchart as an
        image with no searchable structure. If that happened before chunking and embedding, later retrieval could
        return a passage that looked relevant while missing the condition needed to interpret it correctly.
      </p>
      <p>
        I built and tested a structure-preserving RAG ingestion pipeline around those failure modes. MinerU handled
        structured PDF parsing for text and tables. A rule-based linker reattached cross-page notes to the sentences
        that cited them. A visual-language model (VLM) reconstructed flowcharts as Mermaid, preserving their nodes,
        labels, and branches in searchable text. The pipeline then continued through chunking, embedding, retrieval,
        and generation. The stages below follow one controlled test document end to end.
      </p>

      <h2>Designing the recommendation around the client</h2>
      <p>
        The proof of concept was one part of a consulting recommendation. I compared architecture options against
        the client&rsquo;s existing infrastructure, operating cost, implementation effort, maintenance needs, and
        preference for one AI model. The comparison covered both document-processing quality and how each option
        would fit the operating environment.
      </p>
      <p>
        I tested whether one VLM should handle both tables and flowcharts. The evidence supported a leaner division
        of work. MinerU handled structured text and tables. One VLM handled flowcharts. Deterministic rules handled
        cross-page references where predictable matching mattered.
      </p>
      <p>
        The final recommendation combined the requirements, cost and infrastructure analysis, proof-of-concept
        evidence, technical tradeoffs, and a path from prototype to implementation. It documented both the proposed
        RAG ingestion architecture and why that architecture fit the client&rsquo;s operating constraints.
      </p>

