<template>
  <div class="project-story ai-story">
    <h1 data-scroll-reveal>An AI Interface for Exploring a Personal Archive</h1>

    <article data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="110">
      <p class="lede">
        I built Search, <AiAskTerm />, and <AiViewTerm /> into this website so visitors can explore it from the
        questions they bring, alongside the pages and sections I have organised myself.
      </p>
      <p>
        The site now holds research, projects, work, education, places, photographs, and fragments written at different
        times and for different reasons. Those sections give the material a useful structure, but many questions about a
        person draw on several of them at once. An interest such as learning may appear in research, in something I
        built, in work I have done, and in thoughts written elsewhere. A period of my life may be spread across
        education, travel, photographs, projects, and things I wrote around the same time.
      </p>
      <p>
        I wanted a visitor to be able to follow those connections without first knowing where each piece lived. Search
        finds relevant passages across the site and opens the original source. <AiAskTerm /> brings selected passages
        into a cited response. <AiViewTerm /> gathers a broader set of sources into a page around a question, theme, or
        perspective.
      </p>

      <h2>Search turns the whole site into one navigable space</h2>
      <p>
        Search looks across the site&rsquo;s indexed pages and returns the relevant passage together with its original
        location. A visitor can begin with a company, a project, a place, or a broader question without having to
        decide which section of the site to search first.
      </p>
      <p>
        A name such as HSBC gives Search a strong clue because the same word appears in the source material. Broader
        questions are less straightforward. Someone asking how my interest in learning appears across different work
        may use very little of the wording found in the relevant pages. How should Search keep an exact match strong
        while still finding a passage that expresses the same idea differently?
      </p>
      <p>
        Search therefore keeps two rankings. One follows the words in the query, while the other compares its meaning
        with passages across the site.
      </p>
      <div id="retrieval-before-generation" class="interactive-surface retrieval-surface" data-scroll-pop="true">
        <div class="surface-heading">
          <span>Compare word and meaning matches</span>
          <p>Try the same query across both rankings and see what survives when they are combined. The relevance control underneath changes how strong a semantic match must be before Search keeps it.</p>
        </div>
        <RetrievalLab />
      </div>
      <p>
        The lexical ranking is especially useful when the wording itself matters, as it often does for names, titles,
        places, and technical terms. Semantic retrieval can surface a passage whose wording differs from the query
        while the underlying subject remains closely related. The final result uses both.
      </p>

      <h2>Finding the right material before AI writes</h2>
      <p>
        When the site is built, its public content is divided into searchable passages and embedded with
        all-MiniLM-L6-v2. An embedding represents a passage as a vector that can be compared with other text processed
        by the same model.
      </p>
      <p>
        When someone searches, the browser uses the same model to embed the query. Semantic retrieval ranks the stored
        passages by cosine similarity, while lexical retrieval scores shared terms in the indexed text. Reciprocal-rank
        fusion combines the two rankings, with lexical matches given more weight so a precise name or phrase remains
        strong when it matters.
      </p>
      <p>
        Search also applies a minimum semantic similarity before showing a result. A ranking will always have something
        at the top, even when very little on the site is relevant. The threshold lets weak semantic matches fall away
        instead of filling the result list with increasingly distant material.
      </p>
      <p>
        The passage embeddings are already prepared with the site, so the browser only needs to embed the visitor&rsquo;s
        query and compare it with the stored vectors. The retrieval itself runs locally.
      </p>
      <ImplementationDetails />

      <h3>The embedding map</h3>
      <p>
        The same passage embeddings can also be viewed as a space of material from across the site. During the build,
        UMAP projects the high-dimensional vectors into both two and three dimensions. A seeded layout keeps the
        projection stable across builds unless the underlying content changes.
      </p>
      <div id="shared-memory" class="interactive-surface embedding-surface" data-scroll-pop="true">
        <EmbeddingMap initial-view-mode="3d" />
      </div>
      <p>
        Add a question to the map and you can see roughly where it falls among passages from different parts of the
        site. The map is a visualisation of the embedding space. Search itself continues to rank results using the
        original vectors.
      </p>

      <h2 id="grounded-ask"><AiAskTerm /> turns those passages into an answer you can trace</h2>
      <p>
        When the useful outcome is the source itself, Search is often enough. Some questions leave several relevant
        passages on the screen at once, with one part of the answer in a project, another in Research, and something
        related somewhere else on the site.
