      <p>
        <AiAskTerm /> uses the same retrieval layer to bring those pieces into one response. The selected passages are
        numbered and sent with the visitor&rsquo;s question to the generation service. The language model writes from
        that material and attaches citations to the source passages it uses. Each citation can then take the visitor
        back to the original location on the site.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the retrieval-augmented generation, or RAG, part of the system. Retrieval determines which parts of the
        site are available to the response, while the generation prompt asks the model to keep factual claims grounded
        in the supplied material.
      </p>
      <div class="process-strip" data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="100">
        <div class="process-step">
          <span>01</span>
          <h3>Find</h3>
          <p>Find the passages that can support the question.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-step">
          <span>02</span>
          <h3>Set the scope</h3>
          <p>Give the model a clear set of source passages it can use.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-step">
          <span>03</span>
          <h3>Write</h3>
          <p>Form a response from the selected material.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-step">
          <span>04</span>
          <h3>Cite</h3>
          <p>Connect the response back to the original pages.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        <AiAskTerm /> can also suggest follow-up questions that the available material can support. A visitor can move
        from the answer into one of its sources or continue exploring while keeping the same body of site material in
        reach.
      </p>
      <button type="button" class="story-action" @click="openAsk">
        <span><AiAskTerm /> this site</span><ArrowUpRightIcon />
      </button>

      <h2 id="composed-views"><AiViewTerm /> reshapes the site around a chosen angle</h2>
      <p>
        An <AiAskTerm /> response turns several sources into a smaller amount of generated text. That works well when
        the visitor mainly wants an explanation. Some things on this site carry more of their meaning in the form they
        already have. A project may have an interactive component worth trying, photographs may work better as a
        gallery, and a fragment may matter partly because of the way it was originally written.
      </p>
      <p>
        What if those pieces could remain part of the page?
      </p>
      <p>
        <AiViewTerm /> gathers material from across the site around a question, theme, or perspective, then keeps the
        original pieces inside the resulting page while generated writing connects them. A project demo can remain
        interactive, photographs can remain a gallery, and a fragment or research entry can appear in the form already
        defined by the site.
      </p>
      <p>
        Behind the page, <AiViewTerm /> works with a broader catalogue of material than <AiAskTerm /> and composes
        sections from registered source blocks and generated bridge passages. The source blocks refer back to existing
        site material, which allows the composition to reuse a project, fragment, image group, research entry, or other
        supported component without reducing everything to generated prose.
      </p>
      <p>
        A question about learning could therefore bring together research on human learning, a project that applies AI
        in education, an experience that shaped how I approached the problem, and a fragment written from a different
        context. The question gives those pieces a reason to appear together, while each source remains something the
        visitor can inspect on its own.
      </p>
      <ViewComposeInvitation data-scroll-pop="true" />

      <h2>Keeping a View</h2>
      <p>
        Once a <AiViewTerm /> has been composed, it can also be saved. The page keeps the question that produced it,
